"""
pricing_comparison.py
=====================
Section 4.6 — CDD Call Option Pricing

Replicates Section 4.6 of:
  Barnor, Kampouridis & Kanellopoulos (2026)
  "Machine learning for temperature forecasting in weather derivatives"

Unified pricing engine (all 7 models)
--------------------------------------
"We simulated 10,000 future temperature paths by combining the empirical
out-of-sample forecast error from the test data not seen by any of the
models with the forecasts from each respective model for the horizon of
interest. The OU, AR and Neural Network models can be set up to generate
probabilistic forecasts, but since most of our classical ML models are
deterministic models, we implemented this Monte Carlo simulation approach
to be able to more easily compare the results without over complicated
transformations."
                                         — Barnor et al. (2026), Section 4.6

For every model m and every OOS year t:
  1. Take the model's daily forecast f_{m,t,d} for days d in the window
  2. Take the model's empirical OOS residuals e_{m} = actual − forecast
     (from all OOS years, leave-one-out for pricing accuracy)
  3. Block-bootstrap the residuals to generate N_SIM perturbation paths
  4. T_sim = f_{m,t,d} + eps  →  CDD_sim = sum(max(0, T_sim − T_ref))
  5. Price = exp(−r·TTM) · E[TICK · max(CDD_sim − K, 0)]

Outputs (Table 22 metrics)
---------------------------
  μC, σC, Price, Pay.Mean, Pay.Std, Skew, Kurt, VaR5
  per model × region × horizon

Extension beyond Section 4.6
------------------------------
  Pricing accuracy vs realized payoff (per OOS year):
    pricing_error = model_price − realized_payoff
    bias = mean(pricing_error),  MAE,  RMSE

Notes on assumptions not stated in paper
-----------------------------------------
  Strike K: set to ATM = mean(actual cumulative CDD over OOS years).
             The paper refers to K as the strike but does not specify
             how it is set. ATM is a standard and defensible choice.
  T_ref: 10°C (CDD base). Paper uses T_ref = 18°C for HDD; we use 10°C.
"""

